#1 Why `.sm`?

StructMark is made for quick writing too: plain text, short marks, and direct readability. When a document also needs to be read, checked, transformed, and reused by software, it keeps that writing experience while preserving more structure.

#2 Documents are more than a surface

`.sm` treats a document as structured data. Source text goes through the lexer and parser to become an AST, then moves to an HTML renderer, JSON serializer, or custom tool. The presentation can change without rewriting the content.

| Need | Markdown | StructMark |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Human-readable source | Yes | Yes |
| Stable AST | Parser-dependent | Built into the core |
| Semantic directives | Plugin conventions | Registry and attributes |
| Source diagnostics | Usually external | Part of parsing |
| Safe HTML | Renderer-dependent | Included in the core |

#2 What `.sm` gives you

- One source can produce HTML, AST JSON, or another target.
- Directives, attributes, tables, and references carry meaning instead of relying on CSS classes.
- Errors are retained at parse time with line, column, and diagnostic codes.
- Files remain plain text, so they work with Git, review, diff, and automated tests.
- The renderer escapes text and filters unsafe URLs for public documentation sites.

#2 Where it fits

Documentation sites, knowledge bases, technical specifications, data-rich reports, static site generators, and pipelines that pass content between multiple tools.

This website is an example: its copy lives in `.sm` files and is parsed by the StructMark core during the build.
